'''abamurus''' (ab-a̤-mū′rus), ''n.'' [ML., < ''aba-'' (OF. ''a bas'', down, below) + L. ''murus'', wall.] A buttress, or a second wall added to strengthen another. ''Weale.''

'''aban'''† (a-ban′), ''v. t.'' [< ''a-''<sup>1</sup> + ''ban''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.'', after ME. ''abanne(n)'', < AS. ''ābannan'', summon by proclamation.] To ban; anathematize. See ''ban''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''

{{smaller block|How durst the Bishops in this present council of Trident so solemnly to ''abanne'' and accurse all them that dared to find fault with the same? {{right|''Bp. Jewell'', Works, II. 697.}}}}

'''aband'''† (a-band′), ''v. t.'' [Short for ''abandon''.] '''1.''' To abandon (which see).

{{smaller block|And Vortiger enforst the kingdome to ''aband''. {{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., II. x. 65.}}}}

'''2.''' To exile; expel.

{{smaller block|'Tis better far the enemies to ''aband'' Quite from thy borders. {{right|''Mir. for Mags.'', p. 119.}}}}

